There are three recognized subspecies of the Philippine warty pig:
- _S. p. philippensis_, from Luzon and nearby islands. This subspecies has a grey forward-pointing crown tuft and large warts. It was described in 1886 by Nehring.{{Rp|page=183}}
- _S. p. mindanensis_, from Samar, Leyte, and other islands near Mindanao. It has small warts, and black and red-brown crown tufts. Male's skulls are usually larger than females. It was described in 1897 by Charles Immanuel Forsyth Major, a Swiss zoologist-physician.{{Rp|page=183}}
- _S. p. oliveri_, from Mindoro, was described more recently in 2008 by Colin Groves, a biologist-anthropologist. The description was done based on a collection of three skulls and part of a skin. They have "straw-coloured" gonial tufts, and their black crown tufts are equally mixed with straw-coloured hairs.{{Rp|page=186}}

